1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,270
In 1945, an engineer's candy bar melted in

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:05,440
his pocket. Nothing near him was hot.

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:07,620
And that sticky pocket is the reason

4
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:09,240
there's a microwave in your kitchen.

5
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:10,980
A toy that walks down stairs.

6
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:12,680
A glue that was thrown away.

7
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,600
A pan that nothing sticks to.

8
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,320
And a dirty dish that saved millions of

9
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:18,020
lives.

10
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,440
Seven everyday things nobody meant to

11
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,880
invent. Counting down, from the smallest

12
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:24,700
accident to the one that changed the

13
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:25,080
world.

14
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:27,800
Number seven. The spring that walked.

15
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:30,650
In 1943, Richard James was a naval

16
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:32,190
engineer at a shipyard in Philadelphia.

17
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:34,650
He was working on springs to keep delicate

18
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,270
ship instruments steady at sea.

19
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:38,490
One day, a spring fell off a shelf.

20
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:41,430
And instead of just landing, it stepped

21
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:43,590
down. Coil, by coil.

22
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:45,280
As if it was walking.

23
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:47,160
James wondered if the right spring could

24
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:48,020
do that every time.

25
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:50,460
So he went to work, testing different

26
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:51,850
steels and different coils.

27
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:54,050
His wife Betty searched the dictionary for

28
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:56,270
a name, and found a word that meant

29
00:00:56,270 --> 00:00:57,650
sleek and graceful.

30
00:00:58,570 --> 00:00:59,270
Slinky.

31
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:02,240
In November 1945, they showed it off in

32
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:03,760
a Philadelphia department store.

33
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,700
Four hundred springs, one dollar each.

34
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,970
Sold out in ninety minutes.

35
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:11,240
Hundreds of millions have been sold since.

36
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,380
And that's only number seven.

37
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:17,190
Number six. A walk with a dog.

38
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,240
In 1941, Swiss engineer George de Mestral

39
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,720
came home from a walk in the mountains

40
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:22,420
with his dog.

41
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:24,880
Both of them were covered in burrs.

42
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,460
Most people would just pull them off.

43
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,300
He put one under a microscope.

44
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:31,050
Every burr was covered in hundreds of tiny

45
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:33,330
hooks. And the hooks grabbed anything with

46
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:35,730
a loop. Fur, wool, the threads of his

47
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:36,430
trousers.

48
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:38,250
So he asked a simple question.

49
00:01:38,990 --> 00:01:40,670
What if one strip had hooks, and the

50
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:42,060
other strip had loops?

51
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:43,880
It took him years.

52
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:45,740
Cotton wore out too fast.

53
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,880
Then he tried nylon, and the hooks held.

54
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:50,710
He named it after two French words.

55
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:53,370
Velours, for velvet, and crochet, for

56
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:54,820
hook. Velcro.

57
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,780
It's on your shoes, your jacket and your

58
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,260
bag. All because a dog needed brushing.

59
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:04,150
Number five. The glue that was too sticky.

60
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:06,910
In 1942, during the Second World War,

61
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:08,970
chemist Harry Coover was trying to make

62
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:10,630
clear plastic for gun sights.

63
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:12,710
One family of chemicals stuck to

64
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:13,690
everything it touched.

65
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:15,450
Useless for gun sights.

66
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:17,220
So the idea was put away.

67
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:20,860
Nine years later, in 1951, two chemists on

68
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:22,740
his team were measuring one of those same

69
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:25,410
liquids, in an expensive glass instrument.

70
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:27,210
And the instrument's two glass pieces

71
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:28,850
glued themselves together.

72
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:30,430
For good.

73
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,240
This time, Coover saw it differently.

74
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,260
A glue that needs no heat and no

75
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:37,920
pressure. Just a drop, and a few seconds.

76
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:39,440
The secret is water.

77
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,280
Almost every surface has a thin film of

78
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,120
moisture, and it makes the liquid lock

79
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:45,710
into hard plastic chains.

80
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:47,200
It went on sale in 1958.

81
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:50,380
And it still has the same old problem.

82
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,040
It sticks to fingers.

83
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:56,620
Number four. The can that wasn't empty.

84
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,730
April 1938. Chemist Roy Plunkett was

85
00:02:59,730 --> 00:03:01,510
working with a gas for refrigerators,

86
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,450
stored in small metal cylinders.

87
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:04,970
He opened the valve on one.

88
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:06,470
Nothing came out.

89
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:08,750
The gas seemed to be gone.

90
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:10,850
But the cylinder still weighed about the

91
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:13,670
same. So where did the gas go?

92
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:15,270
He cut the cylinder open.

93
00:03:15,690 --> 00:03:17,730
Inside, the gas had turned into a

94
00:03:17,730 --> 00:03:19,930
slippery, white, waxy powder.

95
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:22,370
Its molecules had joined into long chains,

96
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:23,570
wrapped in fluorine.

97
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:25,590
And almost nothing sticks to it.

98
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:28,590
Not water, not oil, not your fried egg.

99
00:03:28,890 --> 00:03:30,290
It was patented in 1941.

100
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:32,910
Today, it's the coating inside non-stick

101
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:35,550
pans. All from a can that looked empty.

102
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:39,700
Number three. The glass that broke, but

103
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:40,830
didn't shatter.

104
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:44,660
In 1903, French chemist Edouard Benedictus

105
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:46,880
dropped a glass flask in his lab.

106
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:48,580
It cracked all over.

107
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:51,960
But the pieces stayed together, still in

108
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,210
the shape of the flask.

109
00:03:53,510 --> 00:03:55,710
The flask had once held a liquid plastic,

110
00:03:56,010 --> 00:03:57,270
called cellulose nitrate.

111
00:03:57,850 --> 00:04:00,150
It had dried into a thin, clear film

112
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:00,890
on the inside.

113
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:03,560
And that film held the glass together.

114
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:05,520
Cars were new back then.

115
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:07,760
And in a crash, their glass broke into

116
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:09,360
sharp, flying pieces.

117
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,820
So Benedictus pressed a film between two

118
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:15,310
sheets of glass, and patented it in 1909.

119
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:17,710
It was first used widely in the eyepieces

120
00:04:17,710 --> 00:04:19,550
of gas masks, in the First World War.

121
00:04:20,190 --> 00:04:21,210
Then in cars.

122
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:23,410
Today, the windscreen in front of you is

123
00:04:23,410 --> 00:04:24,110
made this way.

124
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:26,370
When it breaks, it cracks like a spider

125
00:04:26,370 --> 00:04:27,870
web, and stays in one piece.

126
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:31,970
Number two. Back to that candy bar.

127
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:33,770
1945, in Massachusetts.

128
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:36,170
Percy Spencer was an engineer building

129
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:38,420
magnetrons, the tubes that power radar.

130
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:40,160
Spencer never finished school.

131
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,320
He taught himself electronics, and became

132
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,120
one of the best radar engineers of the

133
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:44,820
war.

134
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,460
One day, standing next to a working

135
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:48,180
magnetron, he felt something odd.

136
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,250
The candy bar in his pocket had melted.

137
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:53,330
So the story goes, he pointed the tube

138
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:55,510
at popcorn kernels, and they popped.

139
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:57,130
Then at an egg.

140
00:04:57,630 --> 00:04:58,790
It exploded.

141
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,910
The invisible waves were shaking the water

142
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:01,950
inside the food.

143
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:03,770
And all that shaking made heat.

144
00:05:04,090 --> 00:05:05,570
A patent was filed that same year.

145
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:08,330
And the first microwave oven, in 1947, was

146
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:10,290
almost six feet tall and weighed about

147
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:12,410
seven hundred and fifty pounds.

148
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:14,390
It took twenty more years to shrink one

149
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:15,920
small enough for a kitchen counter.

150
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:17,760
His colleagues remembered lots of small

151
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:19,360
tests, not one magic moment.

152
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:21,620
But the melted candy bar is the moment

153
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:22,920
everyone remembers.

154
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:25,330
And number one.

155
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:28,230
The dirty dish that saved millions of

156
00:05:28,230 --> 00:05:28,930
lives.

157
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:31,210
September 1928, London.

158
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:33,810
Alexander Fleming came back from a summer

159
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:35,810
holiday to a messy lab bench.

160
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:38,290
He had left out dishes of bacteria, the

161
00:05:38,290 --> 00:05:39,770
kind that cause nasty infections.

162
00:05:40,290 --> 00:05:42,500
And one dish had grown a fuzzy mould.

163
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,940
All around the mould, the bacteria were

164
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:47,500
gone. A clear ring, where nothing could

165
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:48,200
grow.

166
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,420
The mould was making something that killed

167
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:53,200
bacteria. Fleming called it penicillin.

168
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:54,880
But it was hard to make, and for

169
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,620
years, almost nobody used it.

170
00:05:56,930 --> 00:05:59,530
Then, in 1940, a team in Oxford led

171
00:05:59,530 --> 00:06:01,790
by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain showed it

172
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:02,750
could cure infections.

173
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:05,070
By the end of the war, factories were

174
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:06,720
making it in huge amounts.

175
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:09,220
In 1945, the three of them shared the

176
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:12,000
Nobel Prize. And antibiotics have since

177
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,390
saved many millions of lives.

178
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:15,810
So, the countdown.

179
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:17,490
A spring that walked.

180
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:18,770
Burrs on a dog.

181
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:20,230
A glue that was too sticky.

182
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:21,570
A can that looked empty.

183
00:06:21,910 --> 00:06:23,310
A flask that wouldn't shatter.

184
00:06:23,790 --> 00:06:24,830
A melted candy bar.

185
00:06:24,990 --> 00:06:26,190
And a dirty dish.

186
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:28,360
Nobody planned any of them.

187
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:31,240
Each one just needed someone who stopped,

188
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,900
and asked: wait...

189
00:06:33,580 --> 00:06:34,330
why did that happen?

190
00:06:34,810 --> 00:06:36,570
Which accident surprised you most?

191
00:06:36,950 --> 00:06:37,930
Tell us in the comments.

192
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,980
And next time you warm up your food...

193
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:41,520
check your pockets.
